---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const SDK = "web/packages/sdk/src/index.ts";

const H = {
  short: "What the app is built from",
  how: "The four packages",
  rules: "The SDK, generated and checked",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      The React app, <code>@ikisci/app</code>, is private. It builds on four packages under <Path
        p="web/packages"
      />: the API client generated from the server's OpenAPI document, the design tokens and basic
      elements, the scientific elements and functions, and the types plugin authors' components are
      written against. Each package exports by entry point, so a page or a plugin takes only what it
      imports (<Adr n="0191" />). A server change that breaks a page fails to compile, because the
      client's types are regenerated from the server and checked for drift.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <Path p="web/packages/sdk" label="@ikisci/sdk" />: <code>openapi-fetch</code> over a schema{" "}
        <code>openapi-typescript</code> makes from <Path p="web/packages/sdk/openapi.json" />. It
        signs requests with a bearer token, or, in Iki's own pages, sets the page's CSRF token on
        every unsafe method, and turns a problem response into an <code>ApiError</code> (<Adr
          n={[27, 29]}
        />, <Path p={SDK} sym="export function createIkiClient(" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <Path p="web/packages/ui" label="@ikisci/ui" />: tokens, the Inter font and three custom
        elements: a button, a page header and the sidebar. No runtime dependencies. This site uses
        its tokens too (<Path p="web/packages/ui/src/button.ts" />, <Path
          p="web/packages/ui/src/page-header.ts"
        />, <Path p="web/packages/ui/src/sidebar.ts" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <Path p="web/packages/science" label="@ikisci/science" />: scientific custom elements and
        functions, each its own entry point: the sequence and alignment viewers, plasmid maps,
        virtual gels, box maps, quantity and sequence inputs, the structure viewer, the iki-wasm
        parsers and more (<Adr n={[87, 174, 191, 202]} />, <Path
          p="web/packages/science/src/sequence-viewer.tsx"
          sym='customElements.define("iki-sequence-viewer"'
        />, <Path p="web/packages/science/package.json" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <Path p="web/packages/component" label="@ikisci/component" />: what Iki gives a plugin's
        component and what it may ask for, as types, with helpers that send its events (<Path
          p="web/packages/component/src/index.ts"
        />). <PageLink to="Plugin frames" /> is where those components run.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In the repository each package also exports its source under the <code>@ikisci/source</code>{" "}
      condition, which the app's Vite build and type checks and the plugin bundler resolve, so
      they read the packages' source rather than their built files (<Path
        p="web/app/vite.config.ts"
        sym='conditions: ["@ikisci/source"'
      />). The release publishes <code>@ikisci/science</code> and <code>@ikisci/ui</code>{" "}
      to npm with provenance (<PageLink to="Releases and images" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="The SDK, generated and checked" open>
        <p>
          <code>make client</code> writes the OpenAPI document from the server, then the
          TypeScript schema from it, and the Python SDK's types too (<Path p="Makefile" sym="client:" />).
          Three checks catch drift: a server test compares the committed document with the live
          schema; <code>make check-web</code> regenerates the TypeScript and diffs it; and a codegen
          test does the same for the Python SDK's types (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_web_app.py"
            sym="def test_the_published_openapi_document_is_current("
          />, <Path p="scripts/check-client.sh" />, <Path
            p="codegen/tests/test_codegen.py"
            sym="def test_the_python_sdk_types_are_current("
          />). The <PageLink to="Routes" /> page on this site is read from the same document.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Plugin components are bundled from source">
        <p>
          A plugin component with source under its plugin's <code>web/</code> imports from these
          packages, and <code>pnpm bundle:plugins</code> bundles it into one self-contained module;{" "}
          <code>pnpm test</code> fails on a stale bundle (<Path p="scripts/bundle-plugin-components.mjs" />,{" "}
          <Path p="package.json" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p="web/app/package.json" />: the app, and what it depends on</li>
        <li><Path p="web/packages" />: the four packages</li>
        <li><Path p="sdk/python" />: the Python SDK, whose types come from the same document</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0027" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0029" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0191" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
